From 153d79dd885ddf9ecf6fb711d15908dc8c4f812e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rohit Thanvi <rohit@nyaya-ai.local>
Date: Wed, 1 Jul 2026 05:13:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Robustness pass: pipeline bugs, LLM/Redis singletons,
 chat spinner, upload polling
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Backend:
- pipeline.py: total_ms was computed as sum of ALL latency_ms values
  including retrieve_total_ms, double-counting sub-timings that were
  already included in retrieve_total_ms. Now explicitly excluded.
- pipeline.py: run_draft hardcoded confidence=0.85 for unverified
  LLM drafts with no retrieval or source checking. Changed to 0.45
  with an explicit confidence_breakdown and an additional warning.
- llm_client.py: singleton creation was not thread-safe — concurrent
  Celery workers could race on the None check and create multiple
  client instances. Added threading.Lock double-check pattern.
- llm_client.py: stream() had no retry loop unlike complete_messages()
  — a single network hiccup during streaming would end the SSE with
  a raw error token and no retry. Added same retry+backoff pattern,
  capped at 30s per attempt.
- redis_client.py: singleton had no reconnect logic — a dropped Redis
  connection stayed broken silently for the app's lifetime. Added ping
  health check before returning cached client, with reconnect on fail.
  Also added asyncio.Lock to prevent concurrent coroutines creating
  multiple pools simultaneously.

Frontend (chat/page.tsx):
- streamChat's onError callback called commitStreamedMessage(), which
  committed the partial/error stream content as an assistant message
  and still left isStreaming=true — permanently disabling the send
  button. onError now calls setStreaming(false) directly and does NOT
  commit the error content as a message.

Frontend (resumableUpload.ts):
- _finalise had a 600s timeout for synchronous ingestion. With the
  new background=true Celery dispatch, finalise returns immediately
  with status:'processing' — the 600s timeout was meaningless and
  the 0 pages/0 chunks response was displayed as the final result.
  Now polls GET /upload/document/{id}/status with stage-aware progress
  reporting until complete (up to 10 minutes), then returns real
  pages/chunks counts.

Frontend (upload/page.tsx):
- Upload result card showed '0 pages · 0 chunks' for background-
  processed uploads before polling completed. Now shows 'Indexing
  in background…' and 'Will be searchable shortly' for processing
  status, only shows counts when they are non-zero.
